course: dictionary
lesson: 10943
title: Wage: money for work
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A wage is the money a worker is paid for their work. It is usually counted by the hour, the day or the week. Wages, in the plural, is the money a person earns, as in «his weekly wages».
explain: Read the clues around the word. «The minimum wage is the lowest pay per hour allowed by law.» The words per hour tell you a wage is often a rate, so you multiply to find the total. If a worker earns £12 an hour and works 8 hours, the total is 8 × £12 = £96.
explain: Wage can also be a verb, and this use is more formal. «They wage a campaign» means they carry on a fight or a plan for a long time. Ask yourself: is this about money for work, or about carrying on a fight? The sentence tells you which.
fact: A wage is the money a worker is paid for their work.
fact: Wages (plural) is the money someone earns, as in «his weekly wages».
fact: «Hourly wage» means pay for each hour worked.
fact: «Minimum wage» is the lowest pay per hour that the law allows.
fact: To find total pay from an hourly wage, multiply the wage by the number of hours.
exercise: A worker earns £12 an hour and works 8 hours. How much is the pay? | *£96 | £20 | £12 | £8
exercise: «Some taxes are taken from wages, called income tax.» What is taken from wages? | *Income tax | Potholes | VAT | Council tax
exercise: «A wage or salary is the money a worker is paid for their work.» What is a wage? | A fight that lasts for ages | *Money paid to a worker for their work | A type of weather | A rubbish bin
test: «They wage a campaign for years.» In this sentence, what does wage mean? | *Carry on a fight or plan for a long time | Get paid for a job | Stop a plan | Spend money quickly
test: «His weekly wages paid for the food and the rent.» What do wages mean here? | The price of the food | The list of jobs in the town | The weather each week | *The money he earns each week
exam: A worker has an hourly wage of £6 and works 10 hours. What are the wages? | £10 | £16 | £600 | *£60
